  "title": "Sensory-guided joint-learning MI-BCI study",
  "description": "EEG recordings from 39 healthy, BCI-naive participants performing a longitudinal online motor imagery brain-computer interface study of sensory-guided human-machine joint learning. Participants imagined left-hand, right-hand, bilateral-hand or rest during 1D and 2D cursor control across multiple sessions. Cohorts differed in tactile guidance, EEGNet adaptation, or fixed BCI2000 decoding. Data are 62-channel EEG recorded at 1000 Hz and organized in BIDS with HED annotations.",
  "methods_description": "62-channel EEG was recorded with a Compumedics Neuroscan SynAmps 2/RT amplifier and Quik-Cap at 1000 Hz, with BCI2000 for stimulus presentation and online control. Each regular session contained six runs of 5 s trials with visual cursor feedback, plus a separate initial BCI2000 baseline run. Participants completed 4, 6 or 8 sessions. Online decoding used either a weighted EEGNet, an EEGNet, or the BCI2000 linear classifier, and tactile stimulation was applied in selected cohorts.",
